1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,551
Welcome to activity one: defining system and package requirements.

2
00:00:05,551 --> 00:00:12,196
The accelerator below is a teaching model; its manufacturing architecture has not been selected.

3
00:00:12,196 --> 00:00:15,742
Our deliverable is a traceable requirements table.

4
00:00:15,742 --> 00:00:22,634
State what the system must do, under which conditions, and which evidence will demonstrate success.

5
00:00:22,634 --> 00:00:30,000
An impressive stack of chips cannot replace those three answers.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,002
Begin with the use case.

7
00:00:32,002 --> 00:00:38,247
Our hypothetical accelerator must process a sustained workload, not a one-second demonstration.

8
00:00:38,247 --> 00:00:46,096
Record data size, read and write directions, peak and average demand, allowed latency, and operating duration.

9
00:00:46,096 --> 00:00:51,818
The short tall bars represent bursts; the long plateau represents sustained activity.

10
00:00:51,818 --> 00:00:52,993
Describe both.

11
00:00:52,993 --> 00:01:00,000
An interface's headline rate alone does not describe application performance.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,766
Create four requirement identifiers: bandwidth, thermal performance, physical envelope, and test access.

13
00:01:07,766 --> 00:01:13,546
Every row needs conditions, units, a threshold, a verification method, and an owner.

14
00:01:13,546 --> 00:01:18,784
For bandwidth, specify useful data rate in one direction, not simply fast.

15
00:01:18,784 --> 00:01:22,916
Leave unknown values open and assign responsibility for resolving them.

16
00:01:22,916 --> 00:01:30,000
A proposal, an estimate, and a measured pass are different states.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,754
Consider this teaching example: sixty-four lanes in one direction, each carrying sixteen gigabits per second.

18
00:01:37,754 --> 00:01:41,834
Raw aggregate rate is one thousand twenty-four gigabits per second.

19
00:01:41,834 --> 00:01:50,552
Assume eighty percent combined protocol and workload efficiency; divide by eight to obtain one hundred two point four gigabytes per second.

20
00:01:50,552 --> 00:01:53,849
This is arithmetic, not a standards guarantee.

21
00:01:53,849 --> 00:02:00,000
Verify efficiency, direction, and traffic conditions separately.

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:13,389
Start thermal planning with explicit assumptions: two hundred fifty watts sustained, an effective resistance of zero point one eight kelvin per watt for the complete cooling system, and thirty-five degrees ambient.

23
00:02:13,389 --> 00:02:18,693
A lumped steady-state estimate gives eighty degrees, five below our illustrative limit.

24
00:02:18,693 --> 00:02:21,635
That is not verified peak junction temperature.

25
00:02:21,635 --> 00:02:30,000
Interfaces, airflow, local power, and transients still require modeling and measurement.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,774
Pull back to the board.

27
00:02:31,774 --> 00:02:40,667
Reserve sixty by sixty millimeters and four millimeters of package-body height in this example; budget the cooler and mounting hardware separately.

28
00:02:40,667 --> 00:02:47,679
Trace power into the dies, and specify supply range, voltage-drop budget, and measurement points.

29
00:02:47,679 --> 00:02:51,729
A smaller package does not automatically make a smaller system.

30
00:02:51,729 --> 00:03:00,000
Sockets, capacitors, routing, and service clearances affect the layout.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,949
Before a die becomes inaccessible, decide how faults will be observed.

32
00:03:04,949 --> 00:03:11,053
Review incoming die-test evidence, then plan interconnect and functional tests after assembly.

33
00:03:11,053 --> 00:03:16,442
The probes symbolize controllable, observable access; their size is illustrative.

34
00:03:16,442 --> 00:03:20,151
An unobservable internal node cannot simply be assumed good.

35
00:03:20,151 --> 00:03:30,000
Record fault scope, test stage, and data ownership, so expensive integration does not reveal a missing test strategy.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,665
Now compare candidate architectures.

37
00:03:32,665 --> 00:03:39,350
Silicon interposers, fan-out redistribution, and local silicon bridges provide different interconnect routes.

38
00:03:39,350 --> 00:03:42,734
Vertical hybrid bonding can also combine with some routes.

39
00:03:42,734 --> 00:03:46,155
Do not turn them into a mandatory serial factory line.

40
00:03:46,155 --> 00:03:53,247
Compare useful bandwidth, power, dimensions, test access, supply conditions, and total cost.

41
00:03:53,247 --> 00:04:00,000
Keep alternatives open while identifying the evidence needed to distinguish them.

42
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,125
Challenge the cooling assumption.

43
00:04:02,125 --> 00:04:11,735
If effective system resistance rises to zero point two two kelvin per watt, the same screening calculation reaches ninety degrees, exceeding our example limit.

44
00:04:11,735 --> 00:04:14,996
That is an unresolved risk, not an animated pass.

45
00:04:14,996 --> 00:04:20,579
Preserve model revision, boundary conditions, measurement locations, and uncertainty.

46
00:04:20,579 --> 00:04:30,000
Then check sustained throughput; any performance reduction from thermal throttling belongs in the verification result.

47
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,366
Return to the complete system.

48
00:04:32,366 --> 00:04:37,121
Deliver a versioned requirements baseline and an explicit open-issues list.

49
00:04:37,121 --> 00:04:44,380
Link each critical requirement to an owner and its verification evidence; unmeasured items remain unverified.

50
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:50,912
Next comes functional and die partitioning, turning requirements into candidate system compositions.

51
00:04:50,912 --> 00:05:00,000
Defining success first gives every later manufacturing and testing activity a clear purpose and a claim to demonstrate.
